    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This podcast was created in fall quarter 2014 for PWR 2CG: Sounds of Stanford, part of the Program in Writing & Rhetoric 2 at Stanford University. The podcast examines the overarching theme of \"transience\" as it applies to digital audio, movie villains, and preservation.\n\nIn the first section of the podcast, Griffin Stoller talks about how music recording has changed over time, and he shows what musicians can do following the advent of digital audio techniques.\n\nIn the second section of the podcast, Sarah Wymer notes that certain movie villains have gained popularity in recent years, and she offers a few explanations.  She also points out the transition from one-dimensional storybook villains to the modern, human villains we see today.\n\t\nIn the third and final section of the podcast, Christina Hung discusses preservation techniques with respect to time.  She mentions that we currently have more items to preserve than we are capable of preserving, and she delves into the resulting ethical dilemmas.\n\nThe three topics converge on one statement with regard to “transience,” which is that all things must change, and whether or not they change for the better is up to us.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
